description:
packages/swf/src/sprite.ts has no handling for SymbolInstance.buttonHandlers in its instance placement branch (lines 584-654 and 737-799). When a button instance on a symbol timeline has on() handlers (i.e., displayObj.buttonHandlers is non-empty), sprite.ts falls through to the normal PlaceObject2 path and emits the reference charId directly — no per-instance DefineButton2 is generated.

By contrast, compile.ts lines 2044-2073 handles this case: it creates a new instCharId, emits a dedicated DefineButton2 tag with the instance's buttonHandlers via encodeDefineButton2(..., displayObj.buttonHandlers), then places that new charId instead of the symbol's shared charId.

Impact: any button instance placed on a symbol (MovieClip) timeline with attached on(release){...} handlers will compile with the wrong charId and the handlers are permanently lost. The button will still be visible (rendered from the parent symbol's DefineButton2), but none of its event handlers will fire.

Fix: in both the isFirst branch (line ~584) and the posChanged branch (line ~737) of sprite.ts, add a parallel check before placement:
  const hasButtonHandlers = !!(displayObj as any).buttonHandlers?.length;
  if (hasButtonHandlers) {
    const sym = doc.library.items.find((i) => i.itemType === 'symbol' && i.id === displayObj.symbolId);
    if (sym && sym.symbolType === 'button') {
      const instCharId = nextCharId();
      const instHoisted: Array<{ tagType: number; body: Uint8Array }> = [];
      const buttonBody = encodeDefineButton2(instCharId, sym, doc, charIdMap, nextCharId, instHoisted, displayObj.buttonHandlers, ...);
      for (const def of instHoisted) hoistedDefs.push(def);
      hoistedDefs.push({ tagType: Tag.DefineButton2, body: buttonBody });
      refCharId = instCharId;
    }
  }

Also add encodeDefineButton2/encodeDefineButtonSound imports from './buttons.js' to sprite.ts.
